#open-question(title: [Is Microaggregate Burden a Severity Marker or a Fixed Trait?])[
In acute critical illness, amyloid-fibrinogen microaggregate burden behaves as a severity marker. Whether it behaves that way in ME/CFS has not been measured.

Among 104 intensive-care patients and 20 controls, microclots defined as amyloid-fibrinogen aggregates were present in 42.3% of patients on admission and in 0% of controls @Schofield2024microclotsDIC. Presence tracked sepsis (52.3% versus 31.7%, $p = 0.044$), was associated with disseminated intravascular coagulation (odds ratio 51.4, but with a confidence interval running from 6.3 to 6721.1, so the magnitude is effectively unbounded), and with 28-day mortality (odds ratio 5.3, 95% confidence interval 2.0 to 15.6). In that setting the aggregate count carries prognostic information, on the strength of one cohort.

No ME/CFS study has tested the same gradient. Cohorts report microclots as present or absent, or as a group mean against controls, without stratifying by symptom severity, post-exertional malaise burden, orthostatic intolerance, or functional capacity. The two possibilities carry different meanings. If burden is a *state marker* that rises with severity, it could serve as an objective severity readout and as a stratification variable for trials. If burden is a *fixed trait* set near illness onset and independent of current severity, it marks a disease subgroup but cannot track change, and it cannot be used to titrate treatment.

*Falsifiable prediction:* A severity-stratified ME/CFS cohort spanning mild, moderate, severe, and very severe disease, measured with a calibrated assay, would discriminate the two readings: a monotonic gradient across strata supports the state-marker reading, and a flat distribution across strata refutes it. A within-patient longitudinal design crossing burden against post-exertional symptom change would test the tracking claim directly.

*Consequence:* If burden tracks severity, microaggregate measurement becomes a candidate objective endpoint for severity and for treatment response. If it does not, the measurement cannot guide treatment intensity and should not be presented to patients as a monitor of their disease activity.

*Severity applicability.* The severe and very-severe strata are both the untested ones and the ones where a severity marker would matter most. Ambulant cohorts cannot answer this question.
] <oq:microclot-burden-severity-restricted>
